The Android app as distributed: EUPL-1.2
----------------------------------------

The Android build (the APK) includes jmulticard 2.1 (es.gob.afirma.jmulticard:jmulticard and
jmulticard-crypto), the Spanish government's smart-card library. Its files offer these licences:

- The 170 source files with a licence header: LGPL-2.1-or-later or EUPL-1.1-or-later.
- 12 files (the PACE secure-messaging classes connection/pace/DO87, DO8E, DO97, DO99 and
  SecureMessaging, plus a few callback and provider classes): GPL-2.0-or-later or
  EUPL-1.1-or-later.
- The files without a header: GPL-2.0 and EUPL-1.1, per the project's Maven metadata (the
  jmulticard-base POM).
- jmulticard-crypto also bundles Bouncy Castle classes, under the Bouncy Castle licence (MIT).

The EUPL is the only licence that every jmulticard file offers, so we use jmulticard under the
EUPL. The headers allow later EUPL versions, and EUPL-1.1 itself lets a work be redistributed
under a later version (article 5). The Android app as a whole is therefore distributed under the
European Union Public Licence, version 1.2 (EUPL-1.2):

- English text: shared/src/commonMain/composeResources/files/licenses/EUPL-1.2.txt
- Every official language: https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/dec_impl/2017/863/oj

Our MIT code stays MIT inside it: you may also use it on its own under the MIT License.

Source code. Whoever gets the APK can get its complete corresponding source:
- this app, with its build scripts: apps/mobile in
  https://github.com/MeLlamoPablo/SpainAssist, for every release we distribute;
- jmulticard: https://github.com/ctt-gob-es/jmulticard (version 2.1), unmodified.

The iOS app (planned) doesn't include jmulticard: it is MIT, plus the licences of its
dependencies. Once our own card layer (PACE and CWA-14890) replaces jmulticard, the Android app
becomes plain MIT too.


